---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<Fragment slot="head-scripts">
  {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="dryer-repair/ge-not-heating"
  eyebrow="GE Dryer Not Heating · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="GE Dryer Runs but Won't Heat? Start With the Vent"
  subtitle="Most GE no-heat calls trace to a clogged vent and a tripped thermostat — and that vent is a fire risk. Here's what to check, what GE's (few) codes mean, the gas-vs-electric difference, and when it's a tech job."
  heroImageTitle="GE Dryer Not Heating Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A GE that won't heat usually has a choked vent and a tripped thermostat.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a GE dryer tumbles but won't heat, the leading cause is a restricted exhaust vent that overheats the dryer and trips a thermostat or the thermal fuse. After the vent come the heating element (the coils on the heater assembly), the cycling and high-limit thermostats, and the thermal fuse. On a gas GE, it's a weak igniter, gas-valve coils, or the flame sensor. Most GE dryers show no error code at all — that's normal for GE, not a dead control. Clean the filter and full vent first; a thermostat that tripped on a blocked vent will just trip again until the airflow is fixed. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Why the vent comes first — and fast:</strong> the lint-clogged vent that stops your GE from heating is a leading cause of house fires. The U.S. Fire Administration counts about 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the number-one factor. A dryer running hot or refusing to heat is worth clearing the same day.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 GE CODES (honest) -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Does a GE dryer show a code?</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Usually not — and that's normal for GE.</h2>

    <p>Unlike Samsung or LG, most GE dryers are <strong>diagnosed by symptom, not by a digital code</strong>. A GE that won't heat typically just runs cold with nothing on the display, and you find the fault by testing the heating parts rather than reading a code off the panel. If your older GE shows no code, that doesn't mean the control board is dead — it's simply how GE dryers work.</p>

    <p>That's begun to change on GE's newest models (roughly 2024 onward), which added a limited set of heater-related codes such as <strong>HE</strong> (heater error) and <strong>E60 through E64</strong>. If your recent GE shows one of those, it points at the heating circuit. Either way — code or no code — the diagnosis is the same order: vent and power first, then the element, thermostats, and fuse. For the full brand-by-brand picture, see our <a href="/services/dryer-repair/error-codes/">dryer error codes</a> guide.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a GE dryer stops heating</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs find them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Restricted vent → tripped thermostat / thermal fuse (most common)</h3>
      <p>A lint-clogged filter or duct chokes airflow, the dryer overheats, and a high-limit thermostat or the one-time thermal fuse trips and cuts the heat. It only stays fixed once the vent that tripped it is cleared.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Heating element / coils (electric models)</h3>
      <p>GE electric dryers heat with coils on a heater assembly. When a coil burns open, the dryer tumbles cold. Tested for continuity (a good coil reads roughly 8 to 15 ohms) and replaced — a tech job inside the cabinet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Cycling and high-limit thermostats</h3>
      <p>GE dryers use thermostats to regulate and cap the temperature. A failed cycling thermostat can leave the dryer cold; the high-limit trips on overheat, again usually from a blocked vent.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Gas models — igniter, valve coils, flame sensor</h3>
      <p>On a gas GE, no-heat usually means the burner isn't lighting: a weak igniter, failed gas-valve solenoid coils, or a bad flame sensor. An igniter that glows then goes out without the gas lighting points to the coils. Gas parts are a tech job — never DIY the gas valve.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clear the vent, reset the breaker.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Clean the lint filter and the full vent.</strong> Wash the filter, clear the hose behind the dryer, and confirm the exterior flap opens. This fixes most no-heat calls — and removes the fire hazard.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset both breaker legs (electric).</strong> Flip the 240V double breaker fully off and on. A single tripped leg tumbles without heat.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm gas supply (gas).</strong> Verify the gas is on and other gas appliances work. Don't touch the gas valve or line.</li>
      <li><strong>Note any code (newer models).</strong> If your recent GE shows HE or an E6x code, mention it when you call — it points at the heating circuit.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch whether it returns.</strong> Heat restored after a vent cleaning = it was airflow. Still cold on a clear vent = a failed part.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> vent cleaning and the breaker check are safe and fix most GE no-heat calls. Do not open the cabinet to test the heating coils, thermostats, or thermal fuse, and never disturb the gas line or valve on a gas dryer. A no-heat that survives a full vent cleaning is the handoff point.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clean vent, still cold? Time for a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the vent is verified clear and the GE still won't heat, the fault is inside: a burned-out heating coil, a failed cycling or high-limit thermostat, an open thermal fuse (plus the vent that tripped it), or on gas the igniter, valve coils, or flame sensor. Our techs verify the vent first, then test the heating circuit and replace only the failed part. These are among the more affordable dryer repairs, so a no-heat GE is usually well worth fixing. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">dryer not heating</a> guide (all brands) and the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/error-codes/">dryer error codes</a> reference. If your GE takes multiple cycles rather than none, that's <a href="/services/dryer-repair/takes-too-long/">dryer takes too long</a> — usually the same vent. A chronically clogged vent deserves a dedicated <a href="/services/dryer-vent-repair/">dryer vent</a> cleaning. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/ge-dryer-repair/">GE dryer repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dryer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/dryer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/irvine/dryer-repair/">Irvine</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">GE dryer not heating: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>GE dryer still cold after cleaning the vent? Call today.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

